problems.problem:
To each positive integer $n$ a non-negative integer $f(n)$ is assigned in such a way that the following conditions are satisfied:
(i) $f(rs)=f(r)+f(s)$;
(ii) $f(n)=0$ whenever the units digit of $n$ is $3$;
(iii) $f(10)=0$.
Find $f(1985)$. Justify your answer.

Let $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$, $p$ and $q$ be non-zero natural numbers satisfying $ad-bc=1$ and $$\frac{a}{b}>\frac{p}{q}>\frac{c}{d}.$$ Prove that:
(a) $q\geq b+d$;
(b) if $q=b+d$, then $p=a+c$.

Let $\mathbb{N}^*=\{1,2,3,\ldots\}$. Find all functions $f:\mathbb{N}^*\to\mathbb{N}^*$ such that:
(i) if $x<y$, then $f(x)<f(y)$;
(ii) $f\big(yf(x)\big)=x^2f(xy)$ for all $x$, $y$ in $\mathbb{N}^*$.

There are $n$ distinct points $A_1,\ldots,A_n$ in the plane, and to each point $A_i$ a non-zero real number $\lambda_i$ has been assigned, in such a way that $$\big(A_iA_j\big)^2=\lambda_i+\lambda_j$$ for all $i$, $j$ with $i\neq j$. Prove that:
(a) $n\leq 4$;
(b) if $n=4$, then $$\frac{1}{\lambda_1}+\frac{1}{\lambda_2}+\frac{1}{\lambda_3}+\frac{1}{\lambda_4}=0.$$

Let $k\geq 2$ and $n$ be positive integers. There are $kn$ boxes in a straight line, and in each box a stone of one of $k$ different colours is placed in such a way that there are $n$ stones of each colour. A swap consists of exchanging two stones that are in adjacent boxes. Find the smallest positive integer $m$ for which it is always possible to achieve, by means of $m$ swaps, that the $n$ stones of each colour are in consecutive boxes, if:
(a) $n$ is even;
(b) $n$ is odd and $k=3$.

Let $A$ and $B$ be two sets such that:
(i) $A\cup B$ is the set of positive integers;
(ii) $A\cap B$ is empty;
(iii) if two positive integers have as their difference a prime greater than $2013$, then one of them is in $A$ and the other in $B$.
Find all the possibilities for the sets $A$ and $B$.

Given a set $X$ and a function $f:X\to X$, we denote, for each $x\in X$, $f^1(x)=f(x)$ and, for each $j\geq 1$, $f^{j+1}(x)=f\big(f^j(x)\big)$. We say that $a\in X$ is a fixed point of $f$ if $f(a)=a$. For each real number $x$, we define $\pi(x)$ as the number of positive primes less than or equal to $x$. Given a positive integer $n$, we say that $f:\{1,2,\ldots,n\}\to\{1,2,\ldots,n\}$ is catracha if $f^{f(k)}(k)=k$ for every $k\in\{1,2,\ldots,n\}$. Prove that:
(a) if $f$ is catracha, then $f$ has at least $\pi(n)-\pi(\sqrt n)+1$ fixed points;
(b) if $n\geq 36$, then there exists a catracha function with exactly $\pi(n)-\pi(\sqrt n)+1$ fixed points.

Prove that there exists a set $C$ of $2020$ distinct positive integers that simultaneously satisfies the following properties:
– when the greatest common divisor of each two elements of $C$ is computed, a list of numbers that are all distinct is obtained;
– when the least common multiple of each two elements of $C$ is computed, a list of numbers that are all distinct is obtained.

Let $P=\{p_1,p_2,\ldots,p_{10}\}$ be a set of $10$ distinct primes and let $A$ be the set of all integers greater than $1$ such that only primes of $P$ appear in their prime factorisation. The elements of $A$ are coloured in such a way that:
a) each element of $P$ has a different colour;
b) if $m,n\in A$, then $mn$ has the same colour as $m$ or as $n$;
c) for any pair of distinct colours $R$ and $S$, there do not exist $j,k,m,n\in A$ (not necessarily distinct), with $j,k$ of colour $R$ and $m,n$ of colour $S$, such that $j$ divides $m$ and $n$ divides $k$.
Prove that there exists a prime of $P$ such that all its multiples in $A$ have the same colour.

Let $\mathbb{R}$ be the set of real numbers. Determine all functions $f:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ that simultaneously satisfy the following conditions:
(i) $f(yf(x))+f(x-1)=f(x)f(y)$ for all $x,y$ in $\mathbb{R}$;
(ii) $|f(x)|<2022$ for every $x$ with $0<x<1$.

Let $n>1$ be a positive integer. Consider a horizontal row of $n$ cells, each cell painted blue or red. We say that a block is a sequence of consecutive cells of the same colour. Arepito the crab is initially standing on the first cell, at the left end of the row. On each turn, he counts the number $m$ of cells belonging to the largest block containing the cell he is on, and does one of the following:
– If the cell he is on is blue and there are at least $m$ cells to his right, Arepito moves $m$ cells to the right;
– If the cell he is on is red and there are at least $m$ cells to his left, Arepito moves $m$ cells to the left;
– In any other case, he stays on the same cell and does not move any more.
For each $n$, determine the smallest integer $k$ for which there exists an initial colouring of the row with $k$ blue cells such that Arepito manages to reach the last cell, at the right end of the row.

Let $\mathbb{Z}^+$ be the set of positive integers. Determine whether there exists a bijective function $f:\mathbb{Z}^+\to\mathbb{Z}^+$ that satisfies all of the following conditions:
(i) $n$ divides $f(1)+f(2)+\cdots+f(n)$ for every positive integer $n$;
(ii) $f(1)+f(2)+\cdots+f(n)\leq n^2$ for every positive integer $n$;
(iii) $f(1)+f(2)+\cdots+f(n)=n^2$ for infinitely many positive integers $n$.
Note: A function $f:\mathbb{Z}^+\to\mathbb{Z}^+$ is bijective if each positive integer appears exactly once in the infinite list $f(1),f(2),f(3),\ldots$.

A sequence $a_1,a_2,a_3,\ldots$ of integers greater than zero is portenha if it satisfies all of the following conditions:
(i) $a_1<a_2<a_3<\cdots$;
(ii) $2a_n-n$ is a perfect square for every integer $n\geq 1$;
(iii) $a_{n-1}+a_n\leq n^2$ for every integer $n\geq 2$.
Determine all portenha sequences.
